ranlib - libc version of /usr/bin/ranlib (ranlib.c 1.1 07/24/82)
=================================================================

Functions of the libc part that differ from the shipped libc.a: see ../../lib/oldlibc/CATALOG.md (usr/bin/ranlib).

ranlib.spec, ranlib.link   the libc part of /usr/bin/ranlib as modules (input of host/tools/unlink.py, result: link
           order); the old modules are `../../lib/oldlibc/<module>/0_<n>.s`, see there. `make` here links `ranlib`
           from the own objects, these modules and the shipped members that are identical with the original
           (extracted from /lib/libc.a); no libc is linked. 24 of 43 modules are old (fseek ftell readsym rdwr system
           fopen printf doprnt ffltioc filbuf flsbuf endopen findiop cnvt length malloc isatty stty swab strncmp strncpy
           goodmagic swap open). The own code differs from the original, so the symbol addresses are in the spec
           (`noown`, `sym`).
readsym.c  _readsym as embedded in ranlib (0x724..0x79b of /usr/bin/ranlib), reconstructed as C: it reads the
           pieces of a long symbol name over the symbol (no "sym++"), which the shipped libc.a readsym does not;
           ranlib's __.SYMDEF depends on that. It is not built any more: the module `readsym` in the old libc is the
           assembler form of the original (`../../lib/oldlibc/readsym/0_1.s`). The C file is kept as documentation.

The start-up code of the original ranlib is the crt0 with the jump over the text "COPR. 1981 87wega3.2." but without
the SCCS stamp in the data (crt0.list has an empty stamp for it).

The built file is not byte-identical with the original (7732 bytes, as the original, but the content differs): ranlib.o
of the original was made by an older cc (own code differs, D1, D2, D4; 6 bytes less text, 6 bytes more data from the
string alignment D12; 4 bytes more bss). The libc part is identical: `tools/cmpins.py ranlib /usr/bin/ranlib 0x1bce 0x1bd4
0x534 0x53a` gives 0 differing blocks. Run on libc.a and slibc.a the built `ranlib` gives the same archive as the
original (except the 4 bytes of the time stamp in the header of __.SYMDEF).
